          ":question/id": "2b361bbe-dab3-5168-9194-9038dc1e2248",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186172084/3778fa3838fb58594688e0e02c62c2aae4f8d27df1e9420771dbd108d8ecfa2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) varies with the vertical coordinate $y$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $y=0$, where it is zero. It is positive when $y>0$ and negative when $y<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(x,y)&=x\\,\\mathbf{i}-y\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ K(x,y)&=x\\,\\mathbf{i}-x\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is negative when $x>0$ and positive when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf{i}-y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf{i}-x\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
